 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Lipschitz Continuity:** As with all SDF combination operators, if the gradients of the two input SDFs are not orthogonal, Lipschitz continuity ($|\nabla d| \le 1$) may be violated, leading to ray marching artifacts like overshooting or jittering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. This is particularly relevant if the material boundary coincides with a geometric feature where surfaces are parallel or nearly parallel.
-*   **Material Assignment in Intersection:** The choice of which material to assign in an intersection (inner vs. outer) is arbitrary without semantic context. The provided implementation [S1, unofficial] assigns the material of the "left" (first) operand in specific cases, which may not match visual expectations for all CSG trees.
+*   **Lipschitz Continuity:** As with all SDF combination operators, if the gradients of the two input SDFs are not orthogonal, Lipschitz continuity ($|\nabla d| \le 1$) may be violated, leading to ray marching artifacts like overshooting or jittering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. If the angle between gradients deviates by more than $30^\circ$, or if surfaces are parallel and close, continuity is lost [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Material Assignment in Intersection:** The implementation assigns the material of the left operand ($m_a$) when the right distance defines the boundary, which may not match visual expectations for all CSG trees [S1, unofficial].
 
